  "strategy": "<# 1. Approach and Rationale\nWe propose a dual-resolution evolutionary computation approach that iteratively optimizes both the continuous function (real values) and its discrete representation (binary spikes) to improve population diversity and exploitation capability. This method balances exploration in the continuous space with constructive binary representation to prevent local optima trapping and leverage known constructions from discrepancy theory.\n\n# 2. Mathematical Specification\n## Representation\nThe function *h* will be represented with two layers:\n1. **Continuous layer**: Smooth real-valued approximation (h_real)\n2. **Binary layer**: 0/1 spike representation (h_bin)\n\n## Variables\n- *n_points*: Total number of samples (continuous case) or fixed configuration duration (binary case)\n- *\u03bb*: Discretization factor (controls the spread between spikes)\n\n## Objective\n*Minimize*: \n    C\u2085 = max\u2096 || h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) \\|  \nfor all shift magnitudes \n\n## Constraints\n- *h(x)* \u2208 [0, 1]\u2200*x*\n- \u222b*h(x)dx* = 1\n\n## Operations\n1. **Information exchange**: Map binary solution to continuous space\n2. **Regularization**: \u03b2 (controls sparsity) + \u03b3 (smoothness term)\n\n## Derivation\nThe binary representation produces configurations with controlled autocorrelation properties (Eq deep and Eq cal   )\n</#>\n<# 3. Implementation Procedure\n## Population Management\n1. Sample initial population as combinations of:\n   - U(0, 1) uniform realizations\n   - Spike patterns (0/1) built from known low-discrepancy constructions\n\n2. Selection:\n   - Competitive selection based on objective value\n   - Preserve diversity by tracking representative patterns\n\n## Optimization Structure\n```python\nclass DualResolutionOptimizer:\n    def __init__(self, n_boundaries, \u03bb, \u03b2_strength, \u03b3_smoothness):\n        self.n_boundary = n_boundaries\n        self.\u03bb = \u03bb\n        self.\u03b2 = \u03b2_strength\n        self.\u03b3 = \u03b3_smoothness\n        self.history = []\n        self.best_score = float('inf')\n        \n    def evolve(self):\n        candidates = generate_candidates()\n        evaluate_all(candidates)\n        select_survivors()\n        create_new_generation()\n        self.history.append(top_candidate)\n        \n    def evaluate_real_individual(self, h_real):\n        # Fast estimation using \u03b2 and \u03b3\n        penalized_overlap = max_overlap + self.\u03b2 * np.linalg.norm(h_real - h_desired)\n        smoothness_penalty = self.\u03b3 * (np.gradient_sum(h_real))\n        return penalized_overlap + smoothness_penalty\n    \n    def generate_binary_spikes(self, h_real):\n        threshold = self.\u03bb * tanh_activation(h_real)\n        return (threshold < 0.5).astype(int)\n```\n</#>\n<# 4. Search Choices and Variations\n## Primary Parameters\n- *n_boundaries*: 256, 512, 1024 for staged resolution increase\n- *\u03bb*: [0.2, 0.8]\n- *\u03b2_strength*: 1.5 (step response analogy)\n- *\u03b3_smoothness*: 0.8\n\n## Strategy tree\n```\nstart ->\ninitial_runtime -> max_time/2 ->\nbinary_evolution[0.2->0.5\u03bb] -> real_evolution[\u03b2=1.5] -> resolution_increase[if_time] -> convergence_check -> \ud83c\udfc1\n```\n\n## Guideline adaptation\nIf no initial_parent:\n    population = generate_spike_population()  # modulated by \u03bb\nElse:\n    blend_cont_int(initial_parent)   to  2*\u03bb\n</#>\n<# 5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n## Training phases\nPhase 1: 0-30% budget: Spiking evolution (\u223c5 population doublings)\nPhase 2: 30-90% budget: Smooth optimization\nPhase 3: 90-100% budget: Progressive resolution doubling\n\n## Monitor\n```python\nif remaining_time_small:\n    reduce_all_parameters()\n```\n\n## Requirements assistant\n<head>\ndef compute_c5(h):  # modified version accounting for binary structure\n    ...\n</head>\n</#>\n<# 6. Validation and Return Contract\n## Fitness checks\n1. Check \u2211h_matches_DOMAIN = 0.5 (both continuous and binary)\n2. Check overlapping_score computes for entire shift space\n3. Compare with parent configuration using penalty function\n\n## Recovery\n```python\n# When time exhausted, return best candidate with penalized score\n```\n\n## Introspection\nThe planning module  \n- uses smooth activation functions  \n- has\ud14c\uc2a4\ud2b8 (smoothness control)  \n- includes iterative crossover arbitration between representations\n\n# Output format: complete population history + final solution vector overlaid with baseline comparisons",
